# Import of the Lush implementation (929 MB on disk, ~99.8% of it saved model
# checkpoints under Training/). Only the `dataseq` ancestry and enough of its
# surroundings to read it are wanted, so the rule is deny-by-default: nothing
# here is tracked unless it is named below.
#
# This file lives in `.scratch/hmm-lush/` rather than the repo root so that each
# import owns its own rules -- see `.scratch/README.md`. (It originally said "so
# that deleting `.scratch/` at the end of the merge removes the rules along with
# what they describe". `.scratch/` is no longer deleted, as of 2026-08-31: it is
# the migration source for `hmm` and `align` 0.1.0 too. The tracked set is now
# re-scoped per package instead, which a per-import policy supports directly.)
#
# The repo-root `.gitignore` still applies underneath these rules, and is what
# keeps the 42 `.DS_Store` files out even inside the re-included directories. Do
# not restate those here; a negation added below could re-admit them by accident.
#
# NOT PHASED, and deliberately. `hmm` is the next migration in PRD 11 order, and
# the tracked set below is already exactly its scope: the whole live library that
# `load-hmm.lsh` loads (`Code/HMMlib/`, `Code/SeqData/`, `Code/Utility/`), the four
# `hmm-train-*` drivers, both `.sds` specimens, and our own `translation/`. There
# is nothing to widen when the `hmm` branch opens -- the `dataseq` merge and the
# `hmm` translation want the same files here, because the container is only
# legible against the trainer that reads it, and that trainer is the next
# translation target. `align` will want nothing from this tree; see
# `.scratch/align-poc/`, whose Phase 3 covers it.
#
# Unlike the MelodyHPO import, this tree carries no `.git` and no nested
# `CLAUDE.md`/`AGENTS.md`, so neither of the two renames recorded in
# `.scratch/README.md` was needed. It does carry CVS control directories under
# `Code/_Old Lisp Code/`, which git does not treat specially and which the
# catch-all excludes anyway.

# --- deny everything at this level -------------------------------------------
/*
!/.gitignore
!/Code/
!/Training/

# Our own writing about the import -- an ANALYSIS.md and anything joining it.
# This negation is easy to forget and fails silently: a file written here without
# it is simply invisible to `git status`, so the work looks committed and is not.
!/*.md

# Our Python transliteration of the Lush sources (goal 3, subgoal 3). Source only:
# `/translation/*` re-excludes the directory's contents so `__pycache__/` and the
# `.pyc` files inside it stay out -- a directory that is excluded cannot have its
# children re-included, which is what makes the single `*.py` negation sufficient.
# The same silent-failure warning as above applies, and it very nearly fired here:
# these files were written, verified to run, and were invisible to `git status`
# until `git check-ignore` was run on them deliberately.
!/translation/
/translation/*
!/translation/*.py

# --- Code: source only, and only the live source -----------------------------
# `*.lsh` and nothing else. This is what keeps out, without naming them:
#   C/**/*.o          compiled objects for two dead architectures,
#                     powerpc-apple-darwin8 and x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu
#   C/*.c             machine output. Every one opens "WARNING: Automatically
#                     generated code ... by the DH compiler" -- these are Lush's
#                     compiler emitting C from the .lsh beside them, not a
#                     hand-written C layer, so they are a build artefact and
#                     teach nothing the .lsh does not say better
#   *.lsh~,           editor backups and a stray "util.lsh copy"
#   "*.lsh copy"
/Code/*
!/Code/*.lsh
!/Code/hmm-test-view

# The loader `load-hmm.lsh` names these three directories and no others; they are
# the whole live library.
!/Code/SeqData/
!/Code/HMMlib/
!/Code/Utility/

# The merge target proper: dsource-seq.lsh (the container), seq-state.lsh (a
# single sequence), format-sds.lsh (the vocabulary builder and serializer).
/Code/SeqData/*
!/Code/SeqData/*.lsh

# The consumer. `dataseq`'s shape here is only legible against what reads it, and
# this is additionally the translation target of the *next* package in PRD order.
/Code/HMMlib/*
!/Code/HMMlib/*.lsh

# Helpers the above libload -- `mkdir-on-need`, `blank-str`, `setqC`, `member-pos`.
# Without them the container's code does not resolve. The `*.lsh` glob also admits
# three `LU-test-*.lsh` files that `load-hmm.lsh` does not load; they are kept
# because they are the only tests that exist anywhere in this tree, and naming
# `util.lsh` and `mc.lsh` individually to exclude them would trade that for nothing.
/Code/Utility/*
!/Code/Utility/*.lsh

# The DH compiler's output for the `dhc-make` list at the foot of `util.lsh`.
# Tracked 2026-09-03, during the `hmm` numeric migration, for a reason only
# visible then: there is no Lush runtime anywhere in this repository, so the
# generated C is the only *machine-checked* statement of what these primitives
# mean. It settled two questions a reading could only have guessed at --
# `(rand 1.0 -1.0)` compiles to `((-1) - (1)) * Frand() + (1)`, so it is uniform
# on (-1, 1] and the reversed bounds are harmless; and `rand-p-vector` *assigns*
# `1 + noise*rand` to each element rather than perturbing what was there. Only
# `util.c`: the two arch subdirectories hold object files and stay out. The
# directory has to be un-ignored before the file can be, since git never descends
# into an ignored directory.
!/Code/Utility/C/
/Code/Utility/C/*
!/Code/Utility/C/util.c

# --- Training: the driver scripts and two dataset specimens ------------------
# `ds-format` runs format-sds over a `.sds` directory; the `hmm-train-*` pair are
# the entry points. Keep all four: the `-nw` ("no window") variants are not
# duplicates -- they drop the GUI and run the topology search as a bare loop
# (`suggest-move` / `keep-model`), which is the clearest statement of it anywhere
# in the tree.
/Training/*
!/Training/ds-format
!/Training/hmm-train-load
!/Training/hmm-train-load-nw
!/Training/hmm-train-new
!/Training/hmm-train-new-nw

# Two `.sds` directories, tracked whole. A `.sds` is not a file but a directory
# -- `_alphabet`, `_alphabet_size`, `_size`, `_seq_sizes`, `_seq_size_max`,
# `_raw_data`, and one `<i>.seq` per sequence -- and it is the only specification
# of the on-disk format that exists, since no document describes it. Tracking one
# *partially* would be worse than not tracking it: `_size` is a count the loader
# trusts, so a directory missing some of its `.seq` files is not a smaller
# example but a corrupt one.
#
#   set11a_dInt.sds   7 files, 16 KB. One sequence, 1449 symbols, and a real
#                     25-symbol alphabet of multi-character strings (`+4th`,
#                     `-8ve`, and bar-quoted `|E. -2nd|`, `|H _|`) -- which is
#                     the case `dataseq` exists to handle.
#   set01z0_100.sds   107 files, 11 KB. A synthetic {a,b} toy, and the only
#                     specimen showing many *ragged* sequences: 100 of them,
#                     lengths 2 to 20, against `_seq_size_max` 20. That raggedness
#                     is what the loader packs into a dense `size x seq_size_max`
#                     int matrix, so this is the evidence for how the original
#                     padded -- the ADR 0011 question this branch has to answer.
!/Training/set01z0/
/Training/set01z0/*
!/Training/set01z0/set01z0_100.sds/
!/Training/set11a_dInt/
/Training/set11a_dInt/*
!/Training/set11a_dInt/set11a_dInt.sds/

# Three saved models, tracked 2026-09-03 as differential-test fixtures for the
# `hmm` numeric migration. This **qualifies the `Training/<all others>` note
# below**, which declined saved checkpoints as "outputs of the algorithm being
# translated, not inputs to it, and unreadable without the model code that wrote
# them". Both halves are false for this narrow purpose. A `.hmm` directory holds
# `transition_p` *and* `state_p`, `output_p` *and* `state_entropies` -- the inputs
# and the outputs of exactly the two computations being ported -- so it is an
# input/output pair rather than an output. And `save-ascii-matrix` writes a
# `.MAT <ndim> <dims...>` header over plain decimals, readable with no model code
# at all, which tracking these demonstrates rather than asserts.
#
# Measured before tracking, on all three: `A = Pt - I; A[0,:] = 1; b = e0`
# reproduces every saved `state_p` to 5e-5, which is the 4-decimal print format's
# own rounding. So they are *not* stale with respect to the manual
# `update-entropy` discipline (`HMMLIB-ACCOUNT.md` §4), and ~1e-3 is the tolerance
# a test can assert. Three model directories, 36 files, 12 apiece: deliberately
# not the whole of `set02a_200/`, which is 287 files and 1.4 MB.
#
#   m001_0001_001.hmm   size 1. Degenerate, and worth having for it: (Pt - I) is
#                       [0], so the row replacement is the entire content of the
#                       solve and a port that omits it fails here first.
#   m001_0005_005.hmm   size 5.
#   m008_0001_008.hmm   size 8.
!/Training/set02a/
/Training/set02a/*
!/Training/set02a/set02a_200/
/Training/set02a/set02a_200/*
!/Training/set02a/set02a_200/m001_0001_001.hmm/
!/Training/set02a/set02a_200/m001_0005_005.hmm/
!/Training/set02a/set02a_200/m008_0001_008.hmm/

# The three matching `.vpath.xls` decodes and the corpus they were decoded from,
# tracked 2026-09-03 for the Viterbi migration. This qualifies the same
# `Training/<all others>` note a second time, and in the same way. `4090 .xls`
# was declined as "outputs of the algorithm being translated, not inputs to it",
# but a `.vpath.xls` is the *output half of an input/output pair* whose input
# half -- the model, and now the corpus -- is tracked beside it. That is the
# criterion the `.hmm` block above already states; the `.xls` sentence had simply
# not been re-read against it. The lesson the header records about the library
# boundary applies to file *types* too: a migration needs whatever makes the
# translation checkable, and that is rarely the code it translates.
#
# `save-viterbi-path` (`hmm-trainer.lsh:712-727`) writes one row per position --
# `Output<TAB>States<TAB>Entropy` -- plus a leading `-` row for the state before
# the first symbol, which is the N+1 geometry of ADR 0015 printed to disk.
# Measured before tracking: concatenating the 200 `.seq` files reproduces each
# file's `Output` column exactly, and the min-sum recurrence of
# `HMMLIB-ACCOUNT.md` §3 reproduces its `States` column at 1269/1269 positions on
# all three models. These are a decode oracle, not a checkpoint.
#
# The `.sds` is tracked whole, for the reason given above at `set01z0_100.sds`:
# `_size` is a count the loader trusts. It is also the only place the symbol
# *names* survive -- `_alphabet` here reads `0<TAB>begin` ... `5<TAB>a`, where the
# same filename inside a `.hmm` directory holds Lush pointer addresses
# (`#$11F50E0`). A saved model does not denote without the corpus that trained
# it: ADR 0001's cost clause and `DEFERRED.md`'s serialization trigger, measured.
#
#   set02a_200.sds            207 files, ~25 KB of content. 200 sequences,
#                             1268 symbols concatenated, 6-symbol alphabet.
#   m001_0001_001.vpath.xls   17 KB. Size 1, so every path is state 0.
#   m001_0005_005.vpath.xls   16 KB. Size 5, `init_state_p = [0,1,0,0,0]`.
#   m008_0001_008.vpath.xls   16 KB. Size 8, and the only fixture where the
#                             delta-seeding defect (§7) changes the decode: at
#                             position 0 the original prefers state 0
#                             (`init_p` 0.3665) to state 5 (0.6335).
!/Training/set02a/set02a_200.sds/
!/Training/set02a/set02a_200/m001_0001_001.vpath.xls
!/Training/set02a/set02a_200/m001_0005_005.vpath.xls
!/Training/set02a/set02a_200/m008_0001_008.vpath.xls

# --- deliberately not tracked, with reasons ----------------------------------
# Named here rather than left to the catch-all so that the reasoning survives.
#
#   Training/<all others>   ~928 MB of saved checkpoints from 2008-2011 training
#                     runs: 8180 `.dot` graphs, 4179 each of transition_p /
#                     state_p / output_p / _training_log / _total_dl, 4090 `.xls`.
#                     Outputs of the algorithm being translated, not inputs to it,
#                     and unreadable without the model code that wrote them.
#                     Seven exceptions are negated above -- three `.hmm`
#                     directories, three `.vpath.xls`, and one `.sds` -- on
#                     evidence that contradicts both halves of that sentence for
#                     the narrow case of the port's test fixtures. That the same
#                     sentence has now been qualified twice, by two different
#                     migrations, is the finding: "output of the algorithm" is
#                     not a reason to decline a file when its inputs are tracked
#                     beside it, because that pairing is what a differential test
#                     is made of.
#   Code/_Obsolete/   An earlier generation of the same HMM library (hmm-bw,
#                     hmm-ss, hmm-sm). Superseded by HMMlib/ and not loaded by
#                     load-hmm.lsh.
#   "Code/_Old Lisp Code/"
#                     A Common Lisp predecessor, interpreted and never compiled.
#                     Reference only, and the owner's judgement is that it holds
#                     nothing the Lush version does not. Carries CVS/ metadata
#                     from its original repository.
